                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "RECOMMENDED\r\nThe Legend of U-96: A Saga of Steel, Pork, and the Atlantic Abyss\r\n\r\nLet me tell you the tale of our third patrol, so you understand what this game truly is. \r\n\r\nWe slipped out of La Rochelle under the cover of a blood-red dawn. Our mission: intercept an Allied convoy heading for Liverpool. For four days, we tracked them through a raging storm that tossed our steel coffin like a toy. The crew was soaked, the diesel fumes were choking, and the cook was threatening a mutiny because a rogue wave smashed our entire supply of fresh sausages. But we pressed on.\r\n\r\nAt 0200 hours, we found them. A wall of shadows on the horizon. \r\n\r\nI ordered a silent approach. We submerged to periscope depth. My target was a massive Type 1 tanker. Through the lens, I watched her cut through the black water. The tension in the command room was thick enough to cut with a trench knife. My weapons officer crunched the geometry, dialed in the gyro angles, and flooded tubes one and two. \r\n\r\n\"Los!\" \r\n\r\nTwo mechanical shrieks tore through the hull. For forty-eight agonizing seconds, we waited in total silence. Then, a distant, muffled *BOOM-BOOM* reverberated through our hull. The tanker erupted into a towering pillar of fire, illuminating the night sky. It was a glorious, cinematic triumph. I was a god of the sea.\r\n\r\nAnd then, the searchlights hit the water. \r\n\r\nThree British destroyers turned on a dime, their bows cutting the foam straight toward us. \"ALARM!\" I screamed. We threw the boat into a steep dive, aiming for 150 meters. The world tilted. Plates smashed. \r\n\r\nThen came the clicking. *Ping... ping... ping...* The enemy ASDIC sonar had us pinned. \r\n\r\nWhat followed was two hours of absolute, heart-stopping terror. The first depth charge barrage exploded directly above us. The lights blew out. Emergency red lamps flickered on. A high-pressure pipe in the electric motor room blew, spraying freezing sea water everywhere. \r\n\r\nThis is where the epic adventure meets the reality of UBOAT:\r\nWhile my chief engineer was heroically patching the hull breach in waist-deep water, my radioman completely lost his mind, curled into a fetal position on the floor, and started weeping because he missed his mother. Meanwhile, the cook—seemingly oblivious to the fact that we were actively imploding—marched into the control room to inform me that we were officially out of coffee and the men's morale was \"critical.\" \r\n\r\nWe sat on the ocean floor for six hours, breathing through oxygen masks, listening to the destroyers prowl above like wolves. I had to personally drag the crying radioman back to his station, order the engineer to slap the batteries with a wrench, and tell the cook to shut up and distribute the emergency chocolate. \r\n\r\nAgainst all historical odds, the wolves eventually lost our scent. We blew the ballast, limped to the surface under a starry sky, and breathed the crisp ocean air. Half the boat was flooded, our engines were held together by duct tape and prayers, and we were eating raw flour. \r\n\r\nIt was the greatest gaming experience of my life.\r\n\r\n10/10. Bought the game to be a legendary naval commander. Stayed to become a high-stress babysitter in a sinking metal tube. \r\n",
